%!PS
% recorddev.ps
%
% The record device: write down what a page asks to paint and replay it at Emit.
%
% Copyright (c) 2013-2016 Michael Joshua Ryan
% SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause

//.internaldict /QUIET known { }{ (loading recorddev.ps...)print } ifelse

% .xpost_RECORD writes down what it was asked to paint instead of
% painting it, and paints the page at Emit by playing what it wrote into
% a device that does paint. What it costs follows the number of marks
% rather than the size of the page (doc/INTERNALS).
%
% The marking methods, Create, GetPix and Destroy are compiled operators
% the driver installs over this dictionary (src/lib/xpost_dev_record.c);
% what is written here is what a record cannot be given from C: the
% keys the class declares, and the page the record makes at Emit.
%
% Each of those bodies runs with a dictionary current -- the device's own,
% or a scratch one where the body wants somewhere of its own to work --
% and opens it through the bracket that takes it off again however the
% body ends, error included (.indict, graphics.ps).
%
% The class declares exactly the five marking methods it records --
% PutPix, BlendPix, DrawLine, FillRect and FillPoly -- and no other
% optional marking method. FillPath would bring it a whole path and the
% clip shape beside it, DrawRect an outlined rectangle, Erase an
% instruction to reset its page: each is a call the record has no entry
% for, and declining all three has every one of them resolved, above the
% device, into the five it does hold. tests/check-device-skeleton.sh
% holds this dictionary to that.
%
% Flush is the one method here that takes no mark. It asks a device to
% put out what it is holding, and what holds this record's pixels is the
% device below it, so the call belongs there: a record answering it for
% itself would answer for a device it is standing in front of. Declaring
% it takes no call away from the record, which is what the rule above is
% about.
%
% A sampled image is the one thing the five are the wrong shape for, and
% it does not arrive as a device method either. The image painter
% (data/paint.ps) writes an image into the rows of a device that keeps
% them and paints one a rectangle per sample into a device that does
% not; a record of the second would cost tens of bytes a sample against
% the one to three bytes a pixel of the page it exists to avoid holding.
% So the painter looks for /.recordimage on the device instead, and
% hands the whole image -- its rows, the transform placing them and the
% colour tables it baked -- to whatever it finds. The driver puts this
% class's there. It is not dispatched as a marking call and takes none
% of them away from the record.
%
% A glyph is the second such thing and is here for the same reason. Text
% reaches a device one pixel at a time -- a fully covered pixel through
% PutPix and a partly covered edge pixel through BlendPix carrying how
% much of it is covered -- which costs a raster nothing and would cost a
% record tens of bytes for every inked pixel of every character on the
% page. So the glyph painter (src/lib/xpost_op_font.c) looks for
% /.recordglyph, and finding it hands over one coverage mask and one
% placement of it. The mask is shared by every placement it is the same
% mask for, so a page of text costs its distinct glyphs and a placement
% apiece. Like the image entry it is not dispatched as a marking call.
%
% /NoOutput keeps the methods the class brought: the device completion
% (data/device.ps) replaces a device's polygon fill with the compiled
% scan conversion, which would paint spans into a device that has no
% pixels to paint into and would leave the polygon unrecorded.

% dict  .bandheight  rows
% How many rows of its page a device holds at once: the page's own
% height where it holds the whole of it, and fewer where it holds a
% band of it.
%
% This is the one place the reading is taken. The band loop below asks
% it to size the raster it plays into, and a program asking what
% happened to its page (CurBandHeight, data/init.ps) asks it too, so
% what is reported is the reading the loop went by rather than a second
% copy of the arithmetic that agrees with it until one of them is
% changed.
%
% Whether the page is banded is the comparison of this against the
% page's own height, which every caller makes for itself: a budget that
% buys the whole page buys no bands, and a device that is asked for one
% band covering the page is a device holding the page whole.
%
% A device with no record behind it is handed its page and holds it, so
% the rows it holds at once are the page.
%
% A record painting less of its page than the page -- the run an imaging
% bounding box reaches, and nothing outside it (/.playrun below) -- has
% that run and no more to hold, so the run bounds the reading here. The
% page's own height does not, by the paragraph above: the callers hold
% the reading to that themselves, and a budget past the page is how they
% are told there are no bands.
currentglobal true setglobal

% dict  .rowbytes  cost
% What one row of the raster a device's page is painted in costs, asked
% of the class that makes it: the class prices a row for Create and it is
% the same row here. The elements a row takes are left out of the answer
% -- a planar row is three of them against a byte per pixel per plane --
% so a row is priced by the pixels, which is what the page is priced by.
%
% The price is what the class says and is not examined here. A class that
% prices no row in bytes is a class no band arithmetic can be done for,
% and each caller doing that arithmetic says so for itself.
%
% One statement, because two callers want it: the band grid divides the
% budget by it, and the saving a band grid buys multiplies the rows not
% held by it. A second copy of the question would agree with this one
% until one of them was changed.
.xpostsys /.rowbytes { % dict  .  cost
2 dict begin
    /dev exch def
    2 dict begin
        /width dev /width get def
        .privatedict dev /.playclass get get /.rowcost get exec
    end exch pop
end } bind put

.xpostsys /.bandheight { % dict  .  rows
5 dict begin
    /dev exch def
    /ph dev /height known { dev /height get }{ 0 } ifelse def
    /held dev /.playrun known { dev /.playrun get 1 get }{ ph } ifelse def
    dev /.playclass known ph 0 gt and {
        /cls .privatedict dev /.playclass get get def
        /rowbytes dev //.xpostsys /.rowbytes get exec def
        % A row whose bytes are past what an integer counts is a row no
        % band holds one of, so there is nothing to divide: the page is
        % asked for whole and refused by the raster that cannot hold it,
        % which is where a page too large to paint has always been
        % refused.
        cls /BandedPage known
            rowbytes type /integertype eq and
            rowbytes 0 gt and {
            dev /.bandbytes get rowbytes idiv
            dup 1 lt { pop 1 } if
            held ph lt { held 2 copy gt { exch } if pop } if
        }{ held } ifelse
    }{ held } ifelse
end } bind put

% dict  .bandsaving  bytes
% What holding this device's page a band at a time saves over holding it
% whole: the raster of the run it paints, less the raster of one band of
% that run. Zero for a page held whole, which saves nothing.
%
% It is the whole of what a device buys by holding its page's marks
% instead of its pixels, and so the quantity a caller weighing the two
% against each other wants.
%
% Both terms are read where the band loop reads them, above: how deep a
% band is from .bandheight, what a row costs from .rowbytes. So the rows
% this says are not held are the rows the loop does not hold, rather than
% a second calculation that agrees with it until one of them is changed.
.xpostsys /.bandsaving { % dict  .  bytes
4 dict begin
    /dev exch def
    /ph dev /height known { dev /height get }{ 0 } ifelse def
    /held dev /.playrun known { dev /.playrun get 1 get }{ ph } ifelse def
    /rows dev //.xpostsys /.bandheight get exec def
    rows held lt
        { held rows sub dev //.xpostsys /.rowbytes get exec mul }
        { 0 } ifelse
end } bind put

% /target w rows  .bandsatpage  bool
% Whether a run of this many rows, at this width, painted by the named
% device, is one the recording class would hold in more than one band.
%
% The rows are the ones the device would hold, which is the page's own
% height for a page held whole and the run an imaging bounding box
% reaches for a page that declares one -- the caller says which
% (.devicefor below).
%
% Asked of the arithmetic above, over a dictionary standing for the
% device that would be made rather than one that was: the width prices a
% row of the target's raster, the budget says how many of those rows a
% band holds, and a run with more rows than that is a run that bands.
% Asking the one reading rather than restating the comparison is what
% keeps the route and the loop from disagreeing about where the boundary
% falls.
%
% A target this build cannot make, or one whose class prices no row, is
% answered as banding: the answer decides only which of two ways a page
% is reached, and both of them reach a device that is about to refuse
% the target for the same reason.
.xpostsys /.bandsatpage { % /target w rows  .  bool
6 dict begin
    /ph exch def
    /pw exch def
    /tgt exch def
    /rec .privatedict /.xpost_RECORD get def
    /targets rec /.playtargets get def
    targets tgt known {
        % A class a C driver brings is not in the private dictionary
        % until the driver has been loaded, and pricing a row asks the
        % class. This is the load the maker would do, done first and
        % once: a driver whose class is already there is not loaded
        % again, and a build without it registers no loader and answers
        % nothing here.
        /cls targets tgt get def
        .privatedict cls known not {
            rec /.playloaders get tgt 2 copy known
                { get dup where { pop cvx exec }{ pop } ifelse }
                { pop pop } ifelse
        } if
        .privatedict cls known {
            << /width pw /height ph
               /.playclass cls
               /.bandbytes rec /.bandbytes get >>
            //.xpostsys /.bandheight get exec ph lt
        }{ true } ifelse
    }{ true } ifelse
end } bind put

% /name w h  .devicefor  /name'
% Which device a selection is made through at a page of this extent.
%
% A run selecting a device whose page may arrive a band at a time has
% that selection made through this class (src/lib/xpost_interpreter.c),
% and this is where that is taken back. A record holds a page in less
% memory than the page by never holding the page; a page the budget
% already covers is played into a raster of the whole of it, so its
% marks are written down, walked once and dropped again for a raster
% that was going to be whole regardless. Under the budget the device the
% run asked for is made directly, and the marks of such a page are
% written down nowhere.
%
% What is weighed is the rows the device would hold against the budget,
% and nothing about what is on the page. Those rows are the page's own
% height, and, where the page device has left the run an imaging
% bounding box reaches for the device it is about to make (/.rasterband,
% data/init.ps), that run instead: a device given a box builds a raster
% of the rows the box reaches and no more (PLRM 6.2, data/image.ps), and
% it is that raster the budget is a budget for. So a box that brings the
% raster under the budget is a box that hands the page to the device
% directly -- the argument above, with the run in place of the page,
% since a record would write the marks down and drop them again for a
% raster that was going to be that size regardless. Read here and taken
% by the device that is made, which is the only thing that may take it.
%
% The raster not held is the whole of what a page's own numbers can say,
% and it is the term this rule accounts for. The other
% side of the trade it does not account for at all: the marks the record
% holds, which follow the drawing, and the objects a replay builds
% afresh each time it plays a mark into a band, which follow the drawing
% and the number of bands together. Neither is knowable from a page
% extent, and the second is the larger of the two on a page of paths and
% is paid once per band, so it is largest exactly where the budget buys
% the most bands. So this rule is right about the memory a page gives
% back and silent about what holding that page's marks instead costs.
%
% Asked wherever a device is made: where this run's device is made from
% its selection (.startdevice, data/device.ps), and again for every
% page-device request that makes another (data/init.ps). Both are asked
% at the page the device being made is to hold, so a job that opens at
% one page and declares another is weighed at the page it declared --
% which is how a program states a page at all, and the case the whole
% mechanism is for.
%
% Asked at those two points and nowhere between them. Making a device is
% where a page description has already given up whatever it knew of the
% one before: a page-device request erases the page and replaces the
% device (PLRM 6.2), so nothing has been painted that a change of route
% could lose and nothing has been asked of the device being replaced that
% could go on being true. Between two of them a device is being drawn on
% and answering for itself, and a program that has looked at the one it
% had cannot survive being handed another of a different class -- so the
% route is never re-weighed there.
%
% What that leaves a program unable to do is choose how its marks are
% held, which PLRM Appendix G bars it from: a request is weighed on the
% page it asks for, and the same page is weighed the same way whatever
% the request was trying to arrange.
%
% Only for the selection this run made, and only where the run did not
% name this class itself: a request naming some other device than the
% run's selection is made on that device directly. A record plays into
% the device this run's selection was given banding for, so it is that
% selection, and no other name a request may carry, that this weighs.
%
% And only where that selection named no mode. A mode is a run saying
% which of the two routes it wants and is not the weighing's to
% overrule: -d pgm:band is given to this class and records at any page,
% and -d pgm:whole never reaches this class at all, being left on the
% device it names where the selection is read
% (src/lib/xpost_interpreter.c). So the one mode that arrives here is the
% one that says to band, and a selection carrying it is answered without
% being weighed.
.xpostsys /.devicefor { % /name w h  .  /name'
6 dict begin
    /ph exch def
    /pw exch def
    /nm exch def
    % the rows the device being made would hold of this page
    /held ph def
    graphicsdict /.rasterband 2 copy known { get }{ pop pop null } ifelse
    dup type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or
        { dup length 2 ge }{ false } ifelse
        { 1 get /held exch def }{ pop } ifelse
    /StartDevice //.xpostsys /.hostvalue get exec /record eq
    nm /record ne and
    nm /StartDeviceAsked //.xpostsys /.hostvalue get exec eq and {
        /SUBDEVICE //.xpostsys /.hostvalue get exec (band) eq
            { /record }
            { nm pw held //.xpostsys /.bandsatpage get exec
                { /record }{ nm } ifelse }
        ifelse
    }{ nm } ifelse
end } bind put

% dict  .playdrop  -
% Give up the device a record paints through.
%
% The record makes that device and is the only thing that names it, so a
% record that stops naming one has left a device nothing will ever
% retire: what such a device holds is a raster, which is not virtual
% memory (PLRM 3.7.3) and which the collector therefore neither reaches
% nor gives back.
%
% It is unnamed before it is retired, so a second attempt finds nothing
% rather than a device that has begun to give up its memory. A record
% that has painted through nothing yet has nothing to give up.
.xpostsys /.playdrop { % dict  .  -
1 dict {
    /pd.rec exch def
    pd.rec /.playdev known {
        pd.rec /.playdev get
        pd.rec /.playdev undef
        pd.rec /.playrows undef
        dup /Destroy 2 copy known { get exec }{ pop pop pop } ifelse
    } if
} //.xpostsys /.indict get exec } bind put

% rec page  .playparams  -
% The page-device parameters a program asked of a record, put onto the
% device that paints for it.
%
% A page-device request settles what a device is to do, and its keys are
% stored on the device the request made (data/init.ps). Where that device
% is a record, the device that paints is one the record makes for itself
% out of a class, so a key the program asked for reached the record and
% stopped there and the painter knew only what its class said.
%
% Which names are the request's is read from the request rather than
% listed: what currentpagedevice holds is what was asked for, and what is
% carried across is what the record actually has under those names. So a
% key the page-device machinery keeps to itself is not carried, and
% neither is anything of the record's own that no request named.
%
% They are carried before the completion runs, so that a key the
% completion reads -- the process colour model is one -- is there when it
% looks. A key a device reads as it is created arrives separately, at
% the copy the instance is made by: every route that makes a device
% leaves the request's keys for that copy to take (.classcopydict,
% data/device.ps), .playmake above among them. What this carries is for
% the instance that is past its copy -- a raster being kept, and the
% completion about to read the device it kept.
%
% /Private is the one name skipped. It is the handle a device's own state
% is reached through, issued to the instance holding that state, and a
% request naming it would otherwise hand the painter the record's.
.xpostsys /.playparams { % rec page  .  -
2 dict {
    /pp.page exch def
    /pp.rec exch def
    graphicsdict /.pagedevice 2 copy known {
        get {
            pop
            dup /Private eq
                { pop }
                { dup pp.rec exch known
                    { dup pp.rec exch get pp.page 3 1 roll put }
                    { pop }
                  ifelse }
            ifelse
        } forall
    }{ pop pop } ifelse
} //.xpostsys /.indict get exec } bind put
setglobal

/.xpost_RECORD <<
    /dimensions [0 0]
    /NoOutput true  % write the marks down; never rasterize

    % Which device paints this record's page, by the name a run selects
    % it with: -d pgm and -d pgm:band ask for a page played into the
    % grayscale raster, and -d record, which names this class and no
    % device, for the colour one. The driver reads the selection at load
    % and settles the class from this (src/lib/xpost_dev_record.c);
    % .playpage below builds it.
    %
    % A device is here where a record can be played into it, which asks
    % one thing of it: what it shows for a colour must be settled by the
    % colour and by state the record carries, so that a mark written
    % down when it was painted paints the same when it is played. The
    % bilevel class is the one that needed state carrying -- what it
    % stores for a grey is the grey against the threshold under the
    % pixel -- and the record writes the screen down where it changes
    % and puts it back as a replay passes it, so it is a target like the
    % rest.
    %
    % Where the device keeps its pixels is not one of the things asked.
    % A recorded image is not a marking call and is not played back as
    % one: it is written through the writer that wrote it the first
    % time, into the rows of a device that keeps them and through the
    % rectangle fill of a device that keeps a buffer of its own instead
    % -- one call per run of columns, the sampling stated once either
    % way (src/lib/xpost_dev_generic.c). So the compiled writers are
    % targets, and so is anything else offering the five marking methods
    % with a compiled fill among them.
    %
    % Every one of them says its page may arrive a band at a time, which
    % is what the loop below is for; a target that did not would still be
    % played into, in one band covering the page.
    /.playtargets <<
        /pbm /.xpost_PBMIMAGE
        /pgm /.xpost_PGMIMAGE
        /ppm /.xpost_PPMIMAGE
        /tiff /.xpost_TIFFIMAGE
        /png /.xpost_PNGDEVICE
        /jpeg /.xpost_JPEGDEVICE
    >>

    % The driver each of those classes arrives with, where it is a C
    % driver brought in on first use rather than a class a boot file
    % defined. A record is specialised from the class of the device it
    % plays into -- the colour space it records in is that device's --
    % so the driver has to be loaded before the recorder is made, and
    % this is what says which one to load. A target whose class is
    % already there is not named here.
    /.playloaders <<
        /png /loadpngdevice
        /jpeg /loadjpegdevice
    >>

    % A device's features decide what the machinery above it sends, so a
    % record is only faithful where the recorder declares what the device
    % it plays into declares. The driver takes /nativecolorspace, /.ncomp,
    % /TextAlphaBits and /ScreenPaint off the target class and puts them
    % here, rather than this class declaring any of them: a mark carries
    % one value per component of the space it was made in and is played
    % by handing those values to a method whose operands the target's own
    % space decides, so a record made in another space paints a colour
    % nobody named; a target asking for a glyph's partly covered edge
    % pixels as whole pixels would be played the coverage-weighted blends
    % a recorder declaring otherwise was sent; and a target that renders
    % a grey as a pattern of pixels paints each through the threshold
    % under it, which is state a marking call does not carry, so a
    % recorder that did not declare the screen would never be told of one
    % and would replay the page under whichever screen the target held by
    % then.

    /.copydict //.xpostsys /.classcopydict get

    % c1..cN  Ground  -
    % Take up the colour the page is being cleared to. A record holds no
    % pixel to read back, so this is the only statement of what a read
    % off this device answers, and the driver's GetPix reads it here
    % (the driver contract: a read answers the ground wherever a device
    % holds no pixel to answer from). The clearing itself arrives as a
    % rectangle covering the page and is written down like any mark.
    %
    % The record is one array, made once and stored into thereafter, and
    % it is made in global VM: a mark survives the restore that ends a
    % save because raster memory is not part of virtual memory (PLRM
    % 3.7.3), and the ground the page was cleared to has to keep to the
    % page it describes.
    /Ground { % c1..cN IMAGE  .  -
    {
        currentdict /.ground known not {
            currentglobal true setglobal
            /.ground .ncomp array def
            setglobal
        } if
        .ground astore pop
    } //.xpostsys /.indict get exec } bind

    % What the raster a page is played into may cost, in bytes.
    %
    % It is the whole of the bound. A record is priced by the marks and
    % takes any page there is; the raster that paints it is priced by the
    % page, and this is what says how much of that page is held at once.
    % The band is this many bytes' worth of the target's rows, so what a
    % page costs to put out stops following the page's height here and
    % follows this instead.
    %
    % It says one thing more, because the same number is what a page is
    % weighed against to decide whether it is recorded at all
    % (.devicefor above): a page the budget covers is held in one band,
    % which is the page, so it is painted on the device the run asked for
    % and nothing is written down. The two readings are one reading on
    % purpose -- a page is recorded exactly when recording it buys a
    % second band -- and that is why this number has to be large enough
    % to cover the pages that ought not to be recorded.
    %
    % Four million, which is what covers them. The sheets are priced in
    % bytes because that is what this number is, and two byte counts want
    % no unit between them. A page at the default resolution is one pixel
    % to the point, so at three bytes a pixel the largest ordinary sheet
    % there is -- A3 at 842 by 1191 points -- costs 3,008,466 bytes, and
    % every smaller sheet less: letter is 1,454,112 and A4 1,502,970. All
    % three are under this, so all three are held whole and record
    % nothing. Above the sheets the bound is still a bound worth having:
    % a 612 by 40000 strip costs 73,440,000 bytes held whole and is held
    % in 2,178 rows of that instead, giving back all but the budget's
    % worth of it. How deep a band the budget buys follows
    % the width a page is painted at rather than the sheet it is cut to
    % -- 2,178 rows across a letter page at the default resolution, 1,568
    % across the same page at 300 dpi in grey, 522 in colour -- and it is
    % some hundreds of rows at the shallowest of those, which is a band
    % deep enough that the marks reaching two of them are a small part of
    % the marks there are.
    %
    % Four round millions and not the power of two nearest them: a number
    % shaped like a boundary claims to be one, and this is a judgement
    % and not a boundary of anything.
    %
    % The judgement is about the raster and no more, and it is a free
    % parameter. It was put above the largest ordinary sheet's raster and
    % rounded up, and that arithmetic is the whole of where the value
    % came from: no run was weighed to arrive at it and nothing measured
    % what a page costs either way. What is compared against it is a
    % page's own extent, so what a page's marks cost to hold and to play
    % enters neither the comparison nor the choosing, however much of it
    % there is. Where the cost of holding a page whole and the cost of
    % recording it actually cross is unmeasured, and a page whose drawing
    % is far heavier than its sheets suggest is a page this number is not
    % answering for.
    %
    % Four million is what a run that names no budget of its own works
    % to. An invocation may name another, and the one it names is written
    % under this name as that run's device is made (.settlebandbudget,
    % data/device.ps), before the first page is weighed; so what stands
    % here is the budget in force either way, and whichever of the two
    % readings above is taken is taken of that.
    %
    % It is not a page-device parameter and must not become one. PLRM
    % Appendix G declares the band-device setup operators obsolete and
    % bars them from page descriptions, so a program may not choose how
    % its marks are held. What a run chooses instead is the device and
    % this number: selecting a device that bands selects banding,
    % selecting it with the mode that holds the page whole does not, and
    % naming a smaller budget is how a run bands a page four million
    % would cover. Being told what happened is the other thing:
    % currentsystemparams reports the budget and the band a page was held
    % in, and there is no way to write either back.
    /.bandbytes 4000000

    % IMAGE  Flush  -
    % Put out what the device is holding, without ending its page.
    %
    % A record holds marks and no pixels, so it is holding nothing this
    % call is about; what holds this page's pixels is the device it is
    % painted in, and the call belongs there. A record that has painted
    % through nothing yet, and one whose painter takes no such call, has
    % nothing to do.
    /Flush { % IMAGE  .  -
    {
        currentdict /.playdev known {
            .playdev dup /Flush 2 copy known
                { get exec }{ pop pop pop } ifelse
        } if
    } //.xpostsys /.indict get exec } bind

    % IMAGE  .playmake  -
    % The device this record's page is painted in: built, or the one
    % built before kept, and carrying what this record was asked of.
    %
    % Built when the record is made rather than when its first page is
    % put out. A record wraps this device for the whole of its own life:
    % it builds it, it is the only thing that names it, and it gives it
    % up when it is itself retired. A device built at the first page
    % instead would be built inside whatever the job had opened by then,
    % and a save and restore around each page is the ordinary way to
    % write one (data/device.ps). Virtual memory goes back to what the
    % save found, so a device named from the record inside a save is a
    % device the record stops naming at the restore -- holding a raster,
    % which is not virtual memory (PLRM 3.7.3) and which nothing would
    % give back. Built with the record, it is named before any of that,
    % and one raster serves the job.
    %
    % The device built is the class the run selected, named on this one
    % under /.playclass: a mark was written down as it arrived, so what
    % plays it back has to be a device asking for the same colour, and
    % this class was given that device's colour space when it was loaded.
    % It is finished the way any page device is finished, so that the
    % compiled fills it ends up with are the ones the same page painted
    % directly would have had, and what it puts out is therefore the
    % same bytes.
    %
    % One raster for the job's pages, and not one for each of them. A
    % raster comes out of the half of virtual memory nothing here gives
    % back, so a raster per page leaves every page's rows behind and a
    % job of n pages ends holding n pages. So the raster is kept on the
    % record that built it, under /.playdev, and the page after stands on
    % it -- starting each page as a raster fresh from Create would, which
    % is what .moveband does for a run of rows and is asked of it in
    % .playpage below for the same reason. It is built again only where
    % it could not stand for the page in hand: a page of another size, or
    % a band grid of another height, is a different raster and not this
    % one moved, and the raster it replaces is given up before it is
    % replaced.
    %
    % So the page this device can put out is the page a raster can hold,
    % and a band raster holds a taller page than a whole one does. The
    % record itself is priced by the marks and takes any page there is;
    % what refuses a page past what a raster holds is the raster built
    % here.
    /.playmake { % IMAGE  .  -
    16 dict {
        /rec exch def
        /pw rec /width get def
        /ph rec /height get def
        /cls .privatedict rec /.playclass get get def

        % The run of the page's rows this record paints.
        %
        % PLRM 6.2 has a program declare the page's imaging bounding box,
        % asserting that it paints no marks outside it, and leaves a
        % device free to render or to drop the marks that do fall
        % outside. So the rows the box does not reach need not be painted
        % at all: the page shows the ground over them, which is what a
        % device holding its page directly shows over the rows it did not
        % hold, and the loop below has that much less of the page to go
        % over. A page declaring no box is the run of every row it has.
        %
        % The run is left for the device being made by the page device
        % that is making it, and taken here, at the record's Create --
        % which is the only moment it is there to take, the page device
        % having put it about for one device and taken it away again
        % (data/init.ps). The record paints its page long afterwards, so
        % what it took has to be what it kept, and this is where it is
        % kept. Taken, and not merely read: the next device made must not
        % inherit a box that was not asked of it.
        rec /.playrun known not {
            /run graphicsdict /.rasterband 2 copy known
                { 2 copy get 3 1 roll undef }{ pop pop null } ifelse def
            /rtop 0 def
            /rrows ph def
            run type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or
                { run length 2 ge }{ false } ifelse {
                /rtop run 0 get def
                /rrows run 1 get def
                % the run is held to the page, whatever named it
                rtop 0 lt rtop ph ge or { /rtop 0 def } if
                rrows 1 lt { /rrows 1 def } if
                rtop rrows add ph gt { /rrows ph rtop sub def } if
            } if
            rec /.playrun [ rtop rrows ] put
        } if
        /imgtop rec /.playrun get 0 get def
        /imgrows rec /.playrun get 1 get def

        % The rows this raster stands on at once, taken where every
        % reader of it takes it (.bandheight above), and the comparison
        % that says whether the run arrives in more than one of them.
        /bandrows rec //.xpostsys /.bandheight get exec def
        /banding bandrows imgrows lt def
        % ... and the rows it is built with, which is a band of the run
        % where the run arrives in more than one and the whole run where
        % it does not. A budget buying more rows than there are to hold
        % buys the rows there are.
        /rasterrows banding { bandrows }{ imgrows } ifelse def

        % The raster this record is carrying stands for the page where it
        % can stand for it at all: the extent it was built at and the
        % band grid it was built for both have to be this page's, since a
        % raster built for another page holds the wrong rows and there is
        % no moving it onto rows it never took.
        %
        % And it has to be a raster this record still holds. The name is
        % virtual memory and what it names is not: a raster built inside
        % a save and given up when it was replaced leaves this name
        % pointing, after the restore, at a device whose page has already
        % been released. What answers that is state the record keeps
        % outside virtual memory (.playkept).
        /kept rec /.playdev known {
            rec /.playdev get /dimensions get aload pop
            ph eq exch pw eq and
            rec /.playrows get bandrows eq and
            rec rec /.playdev get //.internaldict /.playkept get exec and
        }{ false } ifelse def

        kept {
            /page rec /.playdev get def
        }{
            rec //.xpostsys /.playdrop get exec
            % The run of rows this raster holds is left where a page
            % device leaves one for the device it is about to build, and
            % Create takes it from there. It is the first band of the run
            % the record paints, or the whole of that run where one band
            % covers it; a page declaring no box and needing no band is
            % then every row, which is what Create would have held
            % without being told. Stated in every case, so that the rows
            % this raster holds are the record's answer and not whatever
            % the caller happened to leave behind.
            graphicsdict /.rasterband [ imgtop rasterrows ] put
            % The features of the page-device request this record was
            % made for, left for the class-to-instance copy the painter
            % is made through, which takes them and writes them onto
            % the instance as it is born (.classcopydict) -- so a key
            % the painter's driver reads as the instance is created
            % sees what the request said. The record's own copy kept
            % them on the record under the same name, which is the only
            % reading of them that is there both times this runs: at
            % the record's creation, before the request is stored for
            % currentpagedevice, and at a replay long after.
            % .playparams below still carries the request's keys onto
            % the finished instance, kept rasters taking that route
            % alone.
            graphicsdict /.devrequest
                rec /.devrequest 2 copy known { get }{ pop pop 0 dict } ifelse
                put
            /page pw ph cls dup /Create get exec def
            graphicsdict /.rasterband undef
            % named as soon as it exists, so that a device this record
            % built is a device this record gives up however the rest of
            % this ends
            rec /.playdev page put
            rec /.playrows bandrows put
            rec page //.internaldict /.playbuilt get exec
            % The raster's first move onto a run of rows, made here and
            % not left to the first page. What a device takes up to be
            % able to move a run -- the rows it built, and a row to clear
            % a given-up one against (data/image.ps) -- it takes up at
            % its first move, and that take-up is virtual memory while
            % the rows are not. A first move made while a page is being
            % put out is a first move made inside whatever the job has
            % opened by then: the restore takes the take-up back, the
            % move after it looks for the rows this device built and
            % finds the ones it gave up, and the page after that is
            % painted into rows that hold no pixel. Made here, it is made
            % before the job has opened anything, which is why the raster
            % itself is made here.
            imgtop rasterrows page dup /.moveband get exec
        } ifelse

        % What the program asked of this record, on the device that
        % answers for it, and before the completion below reads any of
        % it.
        rec page //.xpostsys /.playparams get exec

        % and the pages the announcing semantics have counted, which they
        % count on the device they were asked of (data/device.ps) -- this
        % record, and not the device it paints through
        rec /pagenumber known
            { page /pagenumber rec /pagenumber get put } if

        % The finishing every page device takes, so that the compiled
        % fills this one ends up with are the ones the same page painted
        % directly would have had. The record's marks are in device space
        % already, so the scale a completion carries into the default
        % matrix is 1:1 here.
        %
        % Taken again on a raster that is being kept, rather than only on
        % one just built. The completion reads what the device carries --
        % the process colour model is one of the things it reads -- and
        % what it carries is what was just put on it above. A raster
        % built before the program said what it wanted would otherwise
        % keep the answers it was finished with.
        page 1 1 .privatedict /.completedevice get exec

        % How much of its page this record holds at once, which is how
        % much of it the raster above holds: a record has no rows of its
        % own, and what it is asked about them is asked about those.
        rec /.bandrows rasterrows put

        % And what holding the page that way saves over holding it whole,
        % handed down to the device holding the record, which answers it
        % to a caller weighing what the record costs against what having
        % it buys (.recordsaving). A page held whole saves nothing and
        % hands nothing down.
        %
        % Handed down here because this is where the band grid is
        % settled, and again whenever it is settled again, so that the
        % number stands for the grid the run asked for.
        rec dup //.xpostsys /.bandsaving get exec
        //.internaldict /.playsaving get exec
    } //.xpostsys /.indict get exec } bind

    % IMAGE  .playpage  -
    % The page a record holds, painted.
    %
    % Reached from the driver's Emit, which is where a device puts out
    % its page. The record holds marks and no pixels, so the page is
    % made by playing every mark into the device it paints through
    % (.playmake above) and putting out that device's page instead.
    %
    % The name this page goes to has already been settled, on this
    % device, by .transmitpage; it is carried across rather than settled
    % again, since settling it twice would count the page twice against
    % a %d in the name.
    %
    % The page is painted a band at a time where the class says its page
    % may arrive that way and the budget above buys fewer rows than the
    % page has. A band is a raster holding one run of the page's rows: the
    % marks that reach the run are played into it, the rows it holds go
    % out, and the same raster stands for the next run. What is held at
    % once is then the band and not the page, whatever the page's height,
    % and the marks are held instead -- a cost that follows the drawing
    % rather than the paper.
    %
    % One raster, moved, rather than one device per band: a raster comes
    % from the half of virtual memory a restore does not rewind (PLRM
    % 3.7.3), and while the collector reaches that half, a device the
    % page machinery has retired stays reachable and its rows are not
    % given back. So a device per band would leave every band's rows
    % behind and hold the page this exists not to hold. What that costs,
    % and that this does not, is measured rather than argued
    % (tests/run-band-loop-test.sh).
    %
    % A class that has not said its page may arrive in bands is handed
    % the whole of it, which is what such a device expects, and so is a
    % page the budget already covers -- a page put out in one band is a
    % page put out whole, and it is put out the way it always was.
    %
    % What the loop goes over is the run of the page's rows this record
    % paints, which is the run an imaging bounding box reaches where the
    % program declared one (.playmake above). The rows outside it are not
    % stood on, nothing is played into them, and the page shows the
    % ground over them -- which is the page a device holding the same box
    % directly puts out, so declaring a box costs the same rows and the
    % same marks whichever route the page took.
    /.playpage { % IMAGE  .  -
    16 dict {
        /rec exch def

        % The device this page is painted in, and the rows it stands on
        % at once: the raster this record is carrying where it can still
        % stand for the page, and a new one where it cannot.
        rec dup /.playmake get exec
        /page rec /.playdev get def
        /bandrows rec /.playrows get def
        /imgtop rec /.playrun get 0 get def
        /imgrows rec /.playrun get 1 get def
        /banding bandrows imgrows lt def

        rec /.outputfile known { page /.outputfile rec /.outputfile get put } if
        % The colour the page was cleared to, carried onto the raster
        % that paints it. That colour is what a device shows over a row
        % it holds no pixel of, and the raster below holds no pixel of
        % any row outside the band it is standing on, so this is the
        % statement of what the page carries wherever the band loop
        % paints nothing. A record takes it up as the page is cleared
        % (/Ground above); a raster fresh from Create has been told
        % nothing, and one that stood for the page before was told what
        % that page was cleared to.
        rec /.ground known
            { rec /.ground get aload pop page dup /Ground get exec } if
        % The screen the page was painted under, carried onto the raster
        % the same way and for the same reason. A device rendering a grey
        % as a pattern of pixels lays the rows it holds no pixel of under
        % the screen it holds, and a band lays its rows before anything
        % is played into them -- so the raster needs a screen before it
        % stands on its first band, which no entry has yet put there.
        % A record whose target does not screen holds none and this puts
        % nothing.
        rec page //.internaldict /.playscreen get exec

        banding {
            % what the page's emission keeps between bands: the file it
            % is going to and how far down the page it has got. It is
            % this page's and not the page before's, so a raster standing
            % again is given a fresh one.
            page /.bandpage 4 dict put
            imgtop bandrows imgtop imgrows add 1 sub {
                /top exch def
                % the last band is the rows the run has left
                /rows bandrows imgtop imgrows add top sub
                    2 copy gt { exch } if pop def
                /bot top rows add 1 sub def
                % A band that comes to nothing but the ground is not
                % painted at all: the raster is not moved onto its rows,
                % nothing is played into them and they are not put out
                % here. What the page carries there is laid down by the
                % emission that next has rows of its own to write, which
                % covers every row from where the file has reached and
                % lays the ground over each one this device does not
                % hold -- and, where such a band is the last of them, by
                % the call at the end that finds nothing held. So the
                % file gets the rows a painted band would have given it,
                % in the order it has always got them.
                rec top bot //.internaldict /.recordground get exec not {
                    % Every run the raster stands on it is moved onto,
                    % the first of them included: what a move does beyond
                    % choosing the rows is leave them as rows fresh from
                    % Create, and a raster that stood for the page before
                    % needs that of its first run exactly as it needs it
                    % of the rest.
                    top rows page dup /.moveband get exec
                    rec page top bot
                        //.internaldict /.replaypage get exec
                    page dup /Emit get exec
                } if
            } for
            % and the call that holds nothing, which says the page is
            % finished and closes the file behind it
            0 0 page dup /.moveband get exec
        }{
            % A page painted in one band is one run of rows, and a
            % raster standing for the page before still carries that
            % page's ink, so the run is moved onto for this page as a
            % band's is: what a move does beyond choosing the rows is
            % leave them as rows fresh from Create. The first page moves
            % onto it too, where it has nothing to clear away, so that
            % what a page costs is the same on the first as on the rest
            % and a job's memory is flat rather than flat after one page.
            imgtop imgrows page dup /.moveband get exec
            rec page //.internaldict /.replaypage get exec
        } ifelse
        page dup /Emit get exec
    } //.xpostsys /.indict get exec } bind
>> def

//.internaldict /QUIET known { }{ (eof recorddev.ps\n)print } ifelse
